    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> Technology</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> Pros and Cons</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Writing: Productive: Write a diary entry about a significant event. Interactive: Collaborate in writing, with guidance, on a shared text for a group project.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Writing: Can write a diary entry describing a day without using a phone (e.g., "Today, I did not use my phone.").</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Can collaborate with classmates to write a list of activities for a tech-free day (e.g., "I can read a book and play outside.").</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Can write a short paragraph reflecting on an experience without technology (e.g., "I have spent more time with my friends since I stopped using my computer all day long.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Warm-up:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher displays a picture of people engaging in various activities without digital devices (e.g., playing board games, reading books, talking face-to-face in a park). Students are asked to brainstorm and quickly *write* a list of 3-5 activities they *could* do on a day without their cellphone or internet. They *could* write "read a book," "play outside," or "talk to my family."</p>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Modeling:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher models writing a simple sentence on the board using one of the brainstormed ideas and a target modal. For example: "I *could* spend time with my friends." The teacher then highlights the correct use of "could." The teacher models pronunciation of "think" versus "this" as they mention ideas, drawing attention to /θ/ and /ð/ sounds.</p>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What kind of activities *can* you write about for a tech-free day?" (Expected: "Activities without phones or internet.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'could,' does it mean 100% definite or a suggestion/possibility?" (Expected: "A suggestion/possibility.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Technology".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Material:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">A short blog post or diary entry (narrative excerpt) titled "My Tech-Free Saturday" focusing on the pros and cons of disconnecting, incorporating target grammar (modals, comparatives, present perfect) and vocabulary (technology, cellphone, internet, device, social media, solution, benefits, use, reduce, disconnect, interact, save, helpful, meaningful, connected, relaxed, distracted, focused, often, usually, actively).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example excerpt:</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">"October 28th. Today, I did not use my *cellphone* or any *device*! It was a bit *more difficult* at first, as I *usually* check *social media*. But I *could* read a book and *interact* with my family. We played board games for two hours! It felt so *meaningful*. I *have spent more time* with my siblings *since* this morning. I *think technology is useful*, but sometimes we *should reduce* our screen time. Disconnecting *can* be very *helpful*."</p>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Communicative Competence:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Discuss the positive and negative aspects of technology in a guided classroom debate, proposing solutions for overuse.</p>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Comprehension tasks (written):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the text individually and write down two benefits of a tech-free day mentioned in the text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Write "True" or "False" for the following statements: a) The writer *often* uses social media. b) The writer found disconnecting *easier* than expected.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Based on the text, write one suggestion for reducing screen time.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Grammar: Modals ("can," "could," "should"), Comparatives ("easier," "more difficult"), Present Perfect ("since," "for"). Vocabulary: technology, cellphone, internet, device, social media, solution, benefits, use, reduce, disconnect, interact, save, helpful, meaningful, connected, relaxed, distracted, focused, often, usually, actively.</p>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Sentence Completion:</b> Students complete sentences using the target grammar and vocabulary. E.g., "We _______ (reduce / should reduce) our screen time for better health." "Using a physical book is _______ (easy / easier) than reading on a small screen." "I _______ (spend) more time outdoors _______ I _______ (disconnect) from the internet." The teacher monitors and provides individual feedback on written accuracy.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Guided Paragraph Writing:</b> In pairs, students are given sentence starters related to technology pros and cons or a tech-free experience. They write short paragraphs (3-4 sentences) expanding on these ideas. E.g., "I think technology is useful because..." "When I don't use my phone, I can..." "I have found that since I reduced my social media use..." They should aim to incorporate at least one modal, one comparative, and one present perfect structure. Teacher circulates, checking for correct use of forms and vocabulary (e.g., "saved," "disconnected," "liked" - ensuring proper -ed endings).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Peer Check & Feedback:</b> Students exchange their written paragraphs from the previous activity with another pair. They read their classmates' paragraphs and *write* down one positive comment and one suggestion for improvement (e.g., "Your description of being *relaxed* is great. You *could* add more details about *how* you felt *connected*."). This encourages pragmatic functions of giving opinions and offering suggestions in written form.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Technology".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative diary entry and group project text writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part 1: Individual Diary Entry (Productive Writing):</b> Students write a short diary entry (5-7 sentences) reflecting on a day without using a phone or computer (real or imagined). They *should* describe what they did, how they felt, and include at least two of the target grammar structures (modals, comparatives, present perfect) and three vocabulary words (e.g., "Today, I did not use my *device*. It was *more difficult* at first, but I *could* finally feel *relaxed*. I *have spent* the afternoon playing with my dog *since* noon.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part 2: Collaborative List for Group Project (Interactive Writing):</b> In groups of 3-4, students combine their individual experiences and ideas to *collaboratively write* a list of 5-7 alternative activities for a "Tech-Free School Day" group project. For each activity, they *should* add a short sentence explaining its benefit, using modals and vocabulary (e.g., "1. Read a physical book. You *can* focus better, and it's *more relaxing*." "2. *Interact actively* with friends face-to-face. This *could* provide *meaningful* connections."). This list proposes solutions for overuse, linking to the sociolinguistic competence.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Assessment Task:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students submit their individual diary entry and their group's collaborative list of tech-free activities. The teacher will review these for specific language use.</p>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Evaluation Criteria (Teacher):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Content & Relevance:</b> Does the writing address the prompt (tech-free day)? (1-5 points)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Grammar:</b> Correct use of modals (can, could, should), comparatives (easier, more difficult), and present perfect (since, for). (1-5 points)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Inclusion and appropriate use of at least 3 vocabulary words. (1-5 points)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity & Cohesion:</b> Is the text easy to understand? Are sentences well-formed? (1-5 points)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration (for group list):</b> Evidence of shared ideas and coherent group writing. (1-5 points)</li>
                </ul>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"List two new things you *can* now write about technology or a tech-free day."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of writing the diary entry or the activity list was *more difficult* for you?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</b>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How has writing about a tech-free day helped you think about your own *use* of *technology*?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one *solution* or *benefit* you identified in today's writing activities that you *could* try in your daily life?"</li>
                </ul>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Teacher reflection:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The writing activities were well-received, and students actively used the target grammar and vocabulary, especially modals for suggestions. The collaborative list encouraged discussion and diverse ideas. Some students found forming coherent paragraphs using present perfect challenging; more specific sentence combining practice might be needed next time. I will scaffold sentence structure for present perfect using 'since' and 'for' further. </p>
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">Connection to next lesson:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This lesson's writing on "Pros and Cons of Technology" will serve as a foundation for the next lesson (Lesson 5 - Mediation & Integration), where students will integrate these ideas into a larger project, possibly creating a public service announcement or a short presentation based on their reflections and collaborative lists. They will mediate information from their written products to a new format.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>